WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.232 --> 00:00:02.732
(quiet music)

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:07.373
- This is where the shooting took place.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.820
I had just turned 15,

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:15.620
so I was super young,
you know what I mean?

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:18.260
I'm still super young, and it's ridiculous

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:19.390
that I've had to go through

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:20.920
some of the stuff I've had to go through,

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:25.423
but I know that this is
not a game, you know?

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:32.550
People really die from gun violence,

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:34.223
every single day,

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.283
in this neighborhood.

00:00:37.691 --> 00:00:41.441
(sirens wailing in distance)

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:46.670
When you're from Chicago,

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:49.370
you turn on the news and
you see gun violence.

00:00:49.370 --> 00:00:51.010
Somebody shot two blocks away

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:52.360
from where you're at right now.

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:53.340
- [Reporter] Deadly gun violence

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.230
is surging in Chicago once again.

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:58.100
- [Reporter] Two men shot at
crowd during a block party.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.480
- [Reporter] 500 homicides so far.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.780
- But I didn't really feel the gravity

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:07.083
until it was in my living room.

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:12.560
Summer after my freshman
year of high school,

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.350
a young man got shot

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:16.573
across the street from
my apartment complex.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:18.780
- It was like a typical day.

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:21.227
We were going to the
store to get something.

00:01:21.227 --> 00:01:22.905
Got in the car.

00:01:22.905 --> 00:01:24.053
Got to the light.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:26.523
Shots rang off.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:32.223
- [Journey] I hear gunshots,
and I look out my front window.

00:01:32.223 --> 00:01:34.953
- I was shot, made it out of the vehicle,

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:37.575
ran through an alley,

00:01:37.575 --> 00:01:40.150
and ran through the back
of an apartment complex.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:44.600
- My backdoor flies open and in runs Peda.

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.933
He's holding his neck,
bleeding everywhere.

00:01:48.179 --> 00:01:50.350
- [Peda] There was a lot
going through my head,

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.120
a lot of different emotions.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:54.846
- Just asking me over and over again,

00:01:54.846 --> 00:01:56.070
can you help me, can you help me,

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:57.870
I've been shot, and can you help me?

00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:02.913
Lucky for him, I was able to say yes.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:07.110
- Hello, good afternoon.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:10.140
Thank you all so much for having us today.

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:12.150
Like she said, I'm Martine.

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.560
You already know Journey.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.140
We're with Ujimaa Medics.

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:19.140
We want to teach you all what to do

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.280
if you're ever in a situation

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.723
where someone is injured by being shot.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:27.050
- Ujimaa Medics is a
black health collective

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:28.210
based in Chicago

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:33.170
that educates and trains
people on how to handle

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:37.340
and apply first aid to
urban health crises.

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:39.550
- So I'm gonna say a statement.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:42.490
If the statement applies to you, step up

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:44.960
and then back.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.710
I live in a neighborhood
where there's been a shooting.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:52.793
I've heard a gunshot before.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:58.670
I know someone who's been shot.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:05.910
- The ultimate goal is that
we want to get the wounded

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:10.373
to an an ambulance and into a
hospital as soon as possible.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:12.910
We just want to teach people

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:15.560
how maximize that time the most,

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.640
so that way instead of
being filled with panic,

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.120
they can instead be useful to a situation

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.620
and be empowered to help
somebody that they love

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:25.973
or even somebody that
they don't even know.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:29.140
In our gunshot wound first aid training,

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:31.950
we start off by going over
our first aid principles.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.860
- So the first one, can
you all read this for me?

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:37.570
Understand (class joins
in) how they feel--

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:40.800
- Then we go into the
actual physical treatment

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:43.950
of what to do with a gunshot wound.

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:48.200
If you have a wound here that's
bright red spraying blood

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:51.100
and you have a wound
here that's oozing blood

00:03:51.100 --> 00:03:53.560
and it's dark and it's gurgling,

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:55.220
you want to do this one first.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:57.500
How to talk to the wounded,

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:59.610
how to treat them, how to handle them,

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:01.880
how to respect their bodily autonomy,

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:02.713
and things like that.

00:04:02.713 --> 00:04:03.970
- You know I know first aid, right?

00:04:03.970 --> 00:04:05.160
Can I please help you?

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:05.993
- [Journey] Then after that,

00:04:05.993 --> 00:04:08.950
we talk about what to do when
the ambulance gets there.

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:10.260
- You're gonna do the best you can

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:11.430
with what you know how to do

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:13.130
because someone else is coming

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:15.080
to take them to a higher level of care.

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:20.810
- There is a lot of gun and gang violence

00:04:20.810 --> 00:04:23.140
that takes places in the city

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:24.670
but in addition to that,

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:29.610
there is a lack of healthcare
resources in these communities

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:31.950
that are affected the
most by gun violence.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:35.060
Not only will somebody potentially have to

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:38.400
travel a long distance to
get to a trauma center,

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.230
but they will also have
to potentially wait

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:44.393
15, 20 minutes for an
ambulance to get there.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:48.740
- Damian was a young
man that I worked with

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:52.860
and he was killed near the
University of Chicago Hospital

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.340
'cause there was no level
one adult trauma center

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:56.340
on the South Side.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:58.300
He passed away in an ambulance ride

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:00.560
on the way to Northwestern Hospital,

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:03.130
which is 45 minutes across town.

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:06.620
This huge hole was ripped in the community

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:09.380
that really created this tension,

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:11.380
that for people we have to do something.

00:05:13.429 --> 00:05:15.340
- [Crowd In Video] What do
we want? Trauma centers!

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:16.173
When do we want it?

00:05:16.173 --> 00:05:17.430
Now!

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:20.050
- We want a trauma center.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:22.580
We want healthcare.

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:24.730
- And we're not gonna
stop until we get it.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:29.810
- So that began this longterm campaign

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:32.230
for the University of Chicago Hospital

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:34.060
to open a trauma center

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:37.630
and also began U Medics.

00:05:37.630 --> 00:05:42.630
I was literally standing
next to Damian's casket

00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:48.623
at his viewing when the
idea first came to me.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:55.000
- I'm not gonna teach you
how to do anything crazy

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:56.560
or out of your scope.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.570
I'm gonna teach you how
to use first aid skills

00:05:58.570 --> 00:05:59.923
to save somebody's life.

00:05:59.923 --> 00:06:00.756
Okay?

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:02.923
- [Amika] To date,

00:06:02.923 --> 00:06:06.210
U Medics has trained
probably close to 1,500

00:06:06.210 --> 00:06:11.210
and about seven trainers have
used their U Medics training

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:15.200
to help be first responders
in a gunshot wound scenario.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.020
- So she gets shot in the
head, that takes precedence.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:21.605
- We have no illusions
that we are taking steps

00:06:21.605 --> 00:06:25.110
to solve any problem of gun violence.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:27.430
First aid is not a cure-all,

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:30.410
but it changes the way you look at life.

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:33.370
Teaching people how to
care for their own body,

00:06:33.370 --> 00:06:34.940
the bodies of their friends

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:39.940
actually impacts the apathy
that people feel about life

00:06:40.220 --> 00:06:42.833
when you're growing up
in a really violent city.

00:06:44.437 --> 00:06:46.810
- I've never been in a
situation before in my life,

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:47.887
you know what I mean?

00:06:47.887 --> 00:06:49.200
And you've been in that situation before.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:51.620
So this was the first time that I met Peda

00:06:51.620 --> 00:06:53.460
after he got out the hospital,

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:56.140
and I'm basically just explaining to him

00:06:56.140 --> 00:06:58.740
how important it is for his family

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:00.893
to get trained by U Medics.

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:07.300
We trained his entire family,
which is maybe 20 people,

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:11.403
ranging from literally ages 3 to 70.

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:14.560
After I saved Peda's life,

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:18.080
my role in U Medics went into overdrive

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:20.410
because then it just made
me feel like oh my God,

00:07:20.410 --> 00:07:22.960
everybody needs to have this training.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.930
It kind of set a standard for my life

00:07:25.930 --> 00:07:28.950
that was kind of like you have to show up

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:31.250
because look what happens
when you do show up.

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:33.740
We don't want to be doctors.

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:35.320
We don't teach people how to be doctors

00:07:35.320 --> 00:07:36.400
or how to be paramedics.

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:38.470
We want them to get in an ambulance

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:40.823
and get to the hospital
as soon as possible.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:44.490
But how can we keep people from dying?

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:48.040
How can we stop burying our brothers

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:50.483
and our mothers and sisters?

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:54.720
It's not even simply about

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:56.710
getting the gunshot wound training.

00:07:56.710 --> 00:07:59.450
It's just the principle of how
you're gonna carry yourself

00:07:59.450 --> 00:08:02.370
now that you do have this information.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:05.210
And then it also creates a level

00:08:05.210 --> 00:08:10.210
of community accountability,
to say, "I got you."

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:11.513
You're my neighbor.

00:08:11.513 --> 00:08:12.460
Maybe we don't talk every day

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:14.500
and maybe we don't have
anything in common,

00:08:14.500 --> 00:08:17.167
but if you're in need, I can help you out.

